course: dictionary
lesson: 7677
title: Prose and poetry
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Prose is ordinary writing in sentences and paragraphs, like stories, letters and newspaper articles. Poetry is written in lines, often with a rhythm and sometimes rhymes. When the lines stop and start in a way the writer chose, you are probably reading poetry.
explain: The word can also mean a writer's style, the way they put words together. Two writers can both write prose about the same day, yet one may sound funny and the other serious.
explain: «Free verse has no regular rhyme or beat, but the poet breaks the lines where the reader should pause, so it is still poetry, not chopped-up prose.» The clue is the lines. The poet decides where each line breaks, so the text is poetry even without rhyme.
fact: Prose is ordinary written or spoken language in sentences and paragraphs.
fact: Poetry is written in lines and often has a rhythm, and sometimes rhyme.
fact: Stories, articles and letters are all examples of prose.
fact: Novels are prose.
fact: «Prose» can also mean a writer's style: the way they write.
exercise: «Free verse has no regular rhyme or beat, but the poet breaks the lines where the reader should pause.» Is this poetry or prose? | *Poetry | A newspaper article | Prose, because it is written in sentences | A letter to a friend
exercise: Mum writes to her sister in full sentences and paragraphs, with no lines or rhythm. What kind of writing is it? | A song chorus | Poetry | Free verse | *Prose
exercise: From «Free verse has no regular rhyme or beat, but the poet breaks the lines where the reader should pause», copy the word that names writing in sentences and paragraphs. | pause | poetry | *prose | reader
test: Which best describes prose? | A type of drawing | Only spoken words, never written down | *Ordinary writing in sentences and paragraphs | Writing in lines with a strong rhythm and rhyme
test: Why is a poem with no rhyme still poetry? | Because it is very short | Because it has lots of long words | *Because the poet breaks the lines on purpose | Because it is printed in a book
exam: Ella says a novel is poetry because it is long; what is the right answer and why? | Neither, because novels are not written | Poetry, because all long books are poems | *Prose, because a novel is written in sentences and paragraphs, not in lines with rhythm | Poetry, because novels always rhyme
